    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>A commercial property claim is not a bigger homeowners claim. The policy is built from different forms, the payout can be cut by a coinsurance clause, lost income is often a larger number than the physical damage, and one storm can touch a dozen buildings with separate roof sections. Most underpaid commercial claims we see come down to one of those differences being missed or mishandled.</p>
<p>TX Public Adjusting represents the policyholder only. Joseph Dittman (Texas Public Adjuster License #3356839) inspects the property, writes a building-by-building Xactimate estimate, and negotiates the claim with your carrier.</p>

<h2>How commercial claims differ from homeowners claims</h2>
<h3>The policy is a stack of forms</h3>
<p>A commercial package typically combines a property coverage form, a causes-of-loss form, a business income form, and a list of endorsements and schedules. Before anyone argues about the estimate, get the complete policy: the declarations, every form listed, and the statement of values the insurer has on file.</p>
<h3>Coinsurance penalties</h3>
<p>Many commercial policies include a coinsurance clause, often 80%, 90%, or 100%. If the limit you carried was lower than that percentage of the property's value at the time of loss, the insurer can reduce the payment in proportion to the shortfall, even on a partial loss. An agreed value endorsement can suspend the clause. The insurer's figure for the building's value is worth checking rather than accepting.</p>
<h3>Blanket vs. scheduled limits</h3>
<p>A scheduled policy puts a separate limit on each building or location. A blanket limit covers several buildings or locations under one combined amount. Blanket coverage helps when one building is hit much harder than the others, but some blanket policies cap each location through a margin clause or the statement of values.</p>
<h3>Business income and extra expense</h3>
<p>Business income coverage pays lost net income and continuing normal operating expenses while the property is being repaired. Extra expense pays costs you incur to keep operating or reopen sooner, such as temporary space or equipment rental. These are proven with financial records, not photos: profit and loss statements, tax returns, and payroll. Many forms start after a waiting period, and limits vary.</p>
<h3>Period of restoration</h3>
<p>Business income is typically paid for the "period of restoration," which generally ends when the property should be repaired with reasonable speed. Disputes often come down to how long repairs <em>should</em> take when materials or permits are slow.</p>
<h3>Ordinance or law coverage</h3>
<p>Repairing an older building can trigger current-code requirements. Basic commercial forms often exclude or limit those costs. Ordinance or law coverage, usually added by endorsement, can pay for the loss of the undamaged portion, demolition, and the increased cost of construction, if it is on the policy.</p>
<h3>Percentage wind and hail deductibles</h3>
<p>Commercial wind and hail deductibles are often a percentage of the insured value rather than a flat amount. Depending on the wording, that percentage may apply per building, per location, or per occurrence, which can change the deductible dramatically on a multi-building property.</p>
<h3>Multiple buildings and roof sections</h3>
<p>Insurers sometimes sample a few roofs and extend the result to the whole property. Commercial roofs also mix systems on one building, and each section needs its own inspection and line items.</p>
<h3>Tenant vs. landlord responsibilities</h3>
<p>On leased property, the lease decides who insures what. The landlord's policy may cover the shell and roof, while tenants insure their improvements, contents, and income. A loss can produce three or four claims on the same building, and gaps appear when each party assumes the other is covered.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Why documentation and a building-by-building scope matter</h2>
<p>A single summary estimate for an entire property makes it hard to see what was left out. We write the estimate one building and one roof section at a time, tied to photos, drone or roof-level images, Matterport 3D scans where they help, and moisture readings for water losses. When the carrier disagrees, the disagreement is about specific line items on specific buildings, which is far easier to resolve than an argument over a total. Our <a href="/tools/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> covers what to gather before the first inspection.</p>
<p>Storm losses are covered on our <a href="/services/roof-hail-damage-claims/">roof and hail</a> and <a href="/services/hurricane-windstorm-claims/">hurricane and windstorm</a> pages; a low payment already issued is covered under <a href="/services/denied-underpaid-claims/">denied and underpaid claims</a>.</p>

